<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akhetaten</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amarna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akhetaten, known today as Amarna, was the capital city founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a> in the fourteenth century BCE. It was abandoned soon after his death. The site is famous for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>, diplomatic correspondence in Akkadian between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and powers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, which makes it important for the history of several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akhetaten.jpg" alt="Akhetaten" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: en:User:Markh · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Small_aten_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
